# Build context for the three images this repository builds:
# backend/Dockerfile (ticket E0-02), mock-lms/Dockerfile (E0-14) and
# mock-idp/Dockerfile (E0-16). One file serves all three, because Docker reads
# it per build *context* and all three are built from the repository root.
#
# Deny everything, then name what the build needs. The other way round — listing
# what to exclude — fails open: a file added later is in the context until
# someone remembers to exclude it, and `.env` is the file that matters most. It
# is gitignored, so it exists on every developer's machine and in CI, and a
# `COPY . .` would put a real credential into an image layer.
#
# The guarantee is exact, and it is worth stating exactly. At the repository
# root nothing is in the context unless it is named below. Re-including a
# *directory*, though, re-includes everything beneath it — so inside `backend/`
# and `mock-lms/` the deny-all does not apply and the patterns have to be
# re-stated. That is not hypothetical: `.gitignore`'s `.env`, `*.pem`, and
# `*.key` patterns are unanchored, so `backend/.env` is untracked, invisible in
# review, and was reaching the builder stage until the re-exclusions below were
# added. Verified by planting all three and rebuilding.
#
# `mock-lms/` and `mock-idp/` get the same re-exclusions for the same reasons,
# and one more reason of their own: they are the services that hold a signing
# key, so a `*.pem` or `*.key` appearing under either is the exact file E0-14
# criterion 3 and E0-16 criterion 9 say must never exist. Both keys are generated
# per process and never written to disk, so these patterns should never match
# anything — they are here so that the day one does, it does not reach a layer.

*

!pyproject.toml
!README.md
!requirements.txt
!requirements-dev.txt
!backend
!mock-lms
!mock-idp

# Re-excluded inside the directories re-included above. Secrets first: these
# mirror .gitignore, so a file git refuses to track cannot reach a layer either.
backend/.env
backend/.env.*
backend/**/.env
backend/**/.env.*
backend/**/*.pem
backend/**/*.key

# More secret-shaped names, and these do **not** mirror .gitignore: git tracks a
# `.pfx`, a `.p12`, a `.secret`, or a private key called `id_rsa` happily, so the
# sentence above does not cover them. E0-37 item 9 planted a `.pfx` and a
# `.secret` in `backend/app/ai/prompts/`, built the runtime image and listed the
# installed directory inside it: both arrived, where the `.pem` and `.key` lines
# above stop their own suffixes by exactly the same route. The Batch H review
# found the two commonest names that item missed still reaching the image —
# `.p12`, the same format as `.pfx` under its commoner extension, and the
# extensionless `id_rsa` an OpenSSH private key is written as by default. So a
# `signing.p12` or an `id_rsa` parked beside a prompt while debugging was in the
# image, untracked or not.
#
# `id_rsa` has no suffix to glob, so it is matched by its literal basename under
# any directory rather than by an extension.
#
# Only `backend/` needs them. `mock-lms/` and `mock-idp/` hold the signing keys,
# but neither ships package data, so nothing under either is a path into an
# image the way `pyproject.toml`'s `prompts/**/*` glob is.
#
# `scripts/ci/check_image_contents.sh` plants one file per line here, so
# deleting any of these fails the Docker gate naming the line that went.
backend/**/*.pfx
backend/**/*.p12
backend/**/*.secret
backend/**/id_rsa

mock-lms/.env
mock-lms/.env.*
mock-lms/**/.env
mock-lms/**/.env.*
mock-lms/**/*.pem
mock-lms/**/*.key

mock-idp/.env
mock-idp/.env.*
mock-idp/**/.env
mock-idp/**/.env.*
mock-idp/**/*.pem
mock-idp/**/*.key

# Build and test debris; none of it belongs in an image.
backend/**/__pycache__
backend/**/*.py[cod]
backend/*.egg-info

mock-lms/**/__pycache__
mock-lms/**/*.py[cod]

mock-idp/**/__pycache__
mock-idp/**/*.py[cod]

# Editor and merge debris. E0-12 made this reachable: `pyproject.toml` ships
# `app/ai/prompts/**/*` as package data, so any non-hidden file left in that
# directory is copied into the build context, packaged into the wheel, and
# installed into the runtime image. Before that entry the image held installed
# `.py` modules and nothing else. A `validity.v1.md~` or a `validity.v1.md.orig`
# is untracked, invisible in review, and sitting on the machine that runs
# `make docker-build` — so a key parked beside a prompt while debugging ends up
# in an image layer.
#
# The glob stays wide on purpose (ADR 0032: packaging decides what reaches
# production, the naming scheme decides what a file is called, and a glob that
# enforces the scheme does it by making files vanish from containers). This is
# the other end of that trade, and it is **this file's header warning applied in
# the direction that fails open**: a suffix nobody thought of is in the context
# until someone remembers to add it. It is the weaker half of the guarantee, and
# it is here rather than in the glob because narrowing the glob costs a silent
# production failure while missing a suffix costs a stray file.
#
# These four have a gate behind them, and so do `backend/**/*.pem`,
# `backend/**/*.key`, `backend/**/*.pfx`, `backend/**/*.p12`,
# `backend/**/*.secret` and `backend/**/id_rsa` above.
# `scripts/ci/check_image_contents.sh` (E0-36 item 4) plants one file per covered
# pattern, builds the runtime image and looks inside it, so deleting one of those
# ten lines fails the Docker gate in both CI and `make docker-build`, and names
# the line that went. Before that, deleting any of them left every gate green.
#
# An earlier version of this note said the four here were singled out because the
# package-data glob makes this directory a path into the image the other patterns
# do not have. **That was false**, and the `.pem` and `.key` lines are the
# counterexample: same directory, same glob, and measured as excluded by exactly
# those two lines. Believing it is what left them ungated for a ticket, so it is
# recorded rather than quietly replaced.
#
# What is true, and is the reason the `.env` patterns are a different case:
# setuptools' package-data glob skips dotfiles, so `backend/**/.env` does not
# reach the runtime stage by this route at all. It reaches the *builder* stage
# through the build context, which is what those lines are for.
#
# This says nothing about suffixes no line here matches. That is a question about
# what the patterns cover rather than about whether the gate works, and it is not
# answered in this file. **E0-37 item 9 answered two of it** — `.pfx` and
# `.secret`, both measured reaching the image and both now excluded above — and
# **the Batch H review answered two more**, `.p12` and the extensionless `id_rsa`,
# the commonest names item 9 left uncovered, measured reaching the image the same
# way. It left the question itself open: this is still a denylist, and the
# allowlist for this directory that would close it is ADR 0032's trade to reverse
# rather than a line to add here.
backend/**/*~
backend/**/*.orig
backend/**/*.rej
backend/**/*.bak
